So the TGA canRead would likely return false for my XCF file. But the server still ran the TGA fuzzer. Maybe the server just runs the first available fuzzer regardless of the file content?

Actually, looking at the fuzzer code:
```cpp
extern "C" int LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput(const uint8_t *data, size_t size)
{
    int argc = 0;
    QCoreApplication a(argc, nullptr);

    QImageIOHandler* handler = new HANDLER();

    QImage i;
    QBuffer b;
    b.setData((const char *)data, size);
    b.open(QIODevice::ReadOnly);
    handler->setDevice(&b);
    handler->canRead();
    handler->read(&i);

    delete handler;

    return 0;
}
```

The fuzzer always calls `canRead()` and `read()`. The `canRead()` might return false, but `read()` is still called. In the TGA handler's `read()`:
```cpp
bool TGAHandler::read(QImage *outImage)
{
    QDataStream s(device());
    s.setByteOrder(QDataStream::LittleEndian);

    TgaHeader tga;
    s >> tga;
    s.device()->seek(TgaHeader::SIZE + tga.id_length);

    if (s.atEnd()) {
        return false;
    }

    if (!IsSupported(tga)) {
        return false;
    }

    QImage img;
    bool result = LoadTGA(s, tga, img);
    ...
}
```

So even if `canRead()` returns false, `read()` is still called. The `read()` function reads the header and checks `IsSupported`. If the file is not a valid TGA, it returns false. So my XCF file wouldn't trigger anything in the TGA handler.

